WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.106
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEFL preparation.

2
00:00:05.406 --> 00:00:09.269
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.569 --> 00:00:17.504
Unit thirteen: Writing: Email Register and Common Fixes. Today you will choose formal or informal language and correct frequent errors.

4
00:00:17.804 --> 00:00:20.456
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.656
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.517 --> 00:00:26.367
Register.

7
00:00:27.383 --> 00:00:30.021
Register means the level of formality in language.

8
00:00:30.617 --> 00:00:32.152
Choose the right register.

9
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.228
Your turn. Say: Register.

10
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:38.940
Register.

11
00:00:39.683 --> 00:00:40.429
Formal.

12
00:00:41.417 --> 00:00:44.090
Formal means serious and polite in style.

13
00:00:44.683 --> 00:00:47.020
Use formal language for professors.

14
00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:49.863
Your turn. Say: Formal.

15
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:53.573
Formal.

16
00:00:54.317 --> 00:00:55.376
Informal.

17
00:00:56.383 --> 00:00:59.161
Informal means relaxed and friendly in style.

18
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.051
Informal language suits friends.

19
00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.117
Your turn. Say: Informal.

20
00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:08.921
Informal.

21
00:01:09.683 --> 00:01:10.719
Contraction.

22
00:01:11.717 --> 00:01:14.088
Contraction means a short form like don't.

23
00:01:14.683 --> 00:01:17.194
Avoid contractions in formal emails.

24
00:01:17.783 --> 00:01:20.320
Your turn. Say: Contraction.

25
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:24.133
Contraction.

26
00:01:24.883 --> 00:01:25.710
Modal.

27
00:01:26.717 --> 00:01:29.041
Modal means a verb like could or would.

28
00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.023
Could you is polite.

29
00:01:31.617 --> 00:01:33.932
Your turn. Say: Modal.

30
00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:37.675
Modal.

31
00:01:38.417 --> 00:01:39.290
Proofread.

32
00:01:40.283 --> 00:01:42.852
Proofread means to check writing for mistakes.

33
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:44.776
Proofread your email.

34
00:01:45.383 --> 00:01:47.896
Your turn. Say: Proofread.

35
00:01:50.733 --> 00:01:51.653
Proofread.

36
00:01:52.417 --> 00:01:53.534
Concise.

37
00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.608
Concise means short and clear.

38
00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:58.984
Keep your email concise.

39
00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:01.992
Your turn. Say: Concise.

40
00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:06.009
Concise.

41
00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:07.484
Tone.

42
00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:10.471
Tone means the attitude in the writing.

43
00:02:11.083 --> 00:02:12.665
The tone is respectful.

44
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.589
Your turn. Say: Tone.

45
00:02:18.433 --> 00:02:19.167
Tone.

46
00:02:19.967 --> 00:02:24.186
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:29.093
Visal, what is the difference between formal and informal emails?

48
00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:40.638
Formal emails use full forms, polite modals and respectful greetings. Informal emails to friends may use contractions and shorter phrases.

49
00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:43.448
Which phrases are polite?

50
00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:55.820
Could you please send me the notes? is more polite than Send me the notes. I was wondering if you could help is polite and soft, and thank you in advance is a clear closing.

51
00:02:56.917 --> 00:02:58.951
What errors should I fix at the end?

52
00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:09.808
Check subject-verb agreement, tense, articles and spelling. Cut repeated words, and make sure each sentence is concise and clear.

53
00:03:10.967 --> 00:03:12.641
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:13.483 --> 00:03:18.037
Grammar focus: Strategy: match the register, then proofread.

55
00:03:18.337 --> 00:03:19.489
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:20.183 --> 00:03:22.612
Could you please send me the notes from last week?

57
00:03:23.517 --> 00:03:25.748
I was wondering if you could give me more time.

58
00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.705
Choose the register for the reader, use polite modals in requests, and proofread for agreement, tense and spelling.

59
00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:37.301
Be careful! Don't say: I write 'Send me the notes now.'

60
00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:41.617
Say: I write 'Could you send the notes?'

61
00:03:41.917 --> 00:03:43.371
Use polite modals.

62
00:03:44.167 --> 00:03:46.749
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:47.533 --> 00:03:48.860
Fixing an Email.

64
00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:52.750
Sreyneang writes to her manager, Send me the schedule now.

65
00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:56.104
Her teacher says the tone is too direct.

66
00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.713
She changes it to, Could you please send me the schedule?

67
00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.023
She also removes contractions and checks the verb tense.

68
00:04:06.033 --> 00:04:08.915
Her second draft is polite, concise and clear.

69
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.164
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:15.017 --> 00:04:17.028
A formal email avoids blank.

71
00:04:17.328 --> 00:04:21.539
A, closings. B, contractions. C, greetings.

72
00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.250
The answer is B: contractions.

73
00:04:28.417 --> 00:04:29.789
Which is more polite?

74
00:04:30.089 --> 00:04:34.753
A, Help me now.. B, I need help.. C, Could you please help me?.

75
00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.915
The answer is C: Could you please help me?.

76
00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:43.690
To proofread is to blank.

77
00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:48.433
A, check for mistakes. B, write longer. C, translate.

78
00:04:51.967 --> 00:04:54.477
The answer is A: check for mistakes.

79
00:04:55.617 --> 00:04:57.790
Why did the teacher change the sentence?

80
00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:02.707
A, it had no verb. B, it was too direct. C, it was too long.

81
00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:08.893
The answer is B: it was too direct.

82
00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:11.481
A good email is blank.

83
00:05:11.781 --> 00:05:15.933
A, very long. B, angry. C, concise and clear.

84
00:05:19.467 --> 00:05:22.546
The answer is C: concise and clear.

85
00:05:23.733 --> 00:05:27.117
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:27.983 --> 00:05:30.122
How do you make an email polite and correct?

87
00:05:38.917 --> 00:05:40.394
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:40.694 --> 00:05:56.294
I choose the register for my reader, so I use formal language for a professor or manager. I use polite modals like could and would in my requests. Before I finish, I proofread for grammar, spelling and unnecessary words.

89
00:05:56.594 --> 00:05:58.384
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:59.283 --> 00:06:05.059
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: match the register, then proofread.

91
00:06:05.359 --> 00:06:09.973
Today's value is good manners. Say please and thank you, and be polite to everyone.

92
00:06:10.273 --> 00:06:13.007
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

93
00:06:13.307 --> 00:06:16.203
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

